// Designer-guide additions reviewed against Engine/src and Design Department feedback.
const designerWiki = window.GOBO_DESIGNER_WIKI;
designerWiki.groups = [
  {title:'Getting started',pages:['home','template','lifecycle','first-playable']},
  {title:'Your game',pages:['igame','config','context']},
  {title:'Gameplay tools',pages:['entities','actors','characters','systems','assets','input','collision','camera']},
  {title:'Presentation',pages:['ui-audio']},
  {title:'Reference',pages:['limits']}
];
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'lifecycle'), {content:`<pre><code>Configure → engine starts → Start\neach frame: BeginFrame → input → Update → entity changes apply → clear → Render\nStop → engine shuts down</code></pre><p><strong>Configure</strong> runs before the window exists. <strong>Start</strong> runs once when services are ready. <strong>BeginFrame</strong> runs before input, so register listeners there. <strong>Update</strong> receives seconds since the last frame and is where gameplay changes belong. <strong>Render</strong> runs after the screen is cleared. <strong>Stop</strong> runs once during shutdown.</p><p>Entities created or destroyed during Update are applied after Update. Engine does not automatically run your gameplay systems: register them in Start and call their Update functions in the appropriate callback.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'input'), {content:`<pre><code>void Game::BeginFrame(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;KeyPressedEvent&gt;(\n    this, &amp;Game::OnKeyPressed);\n}\nvoid Game::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_ESCAPE) context-&gt;RequestQuit();\n}</code></pre><p>Subscriptions reset each frame before BeginFrame, so register them each frame. The callback owner must remain alive while subscribed.</p><h2>What a key event means</h2><p>KeyPressedEvent reports a key-down symbol only. It does not say whether a key is held, released, repeating, or how long it was down. Use it for one-shot actions such as pause, confirm, or quit.</p><p>For continuous movement, a game can use SDL’s keyboard-state query in its own Update code. This is a lower-level SDL option: include <code>&lt;SDL3/SDL.h&gt;</code>, read <code>SDL_GetKeyboardState(nullptr)</code>, and explicitly set velocity back to zero when no movement key is held.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'limits'), {content:`<h2>What is ready</h2><ul><li>Texture-backed sprites, 2D positions and movement, camera movement, frame animations, AABB collision, events, and entity/component systems are available.</li><li>Windows and the supplied CMake presets are the supported workflow.</li><li>Vector2 is the normal 2D value type. Avoid unchecked indexes and division by zero.</li></ul><h2>Plan around these boundaries</h2><ul><li>There is no Engine-provided text, font, HUD layout, menu, button, or audio API yet.</li><li>KeyPressedEvent is key-down only; it does not provide held, released, repeat, or timing details.</li><li>Collision is rectangle-only today. There are no built-in circles, radius queries, collision layers, or masks.</li><li>Tags and groups should be treated as creation-time labels. Unknown-label lookup and changing labels later are not dependable workflow.</li><li>Damage, keyboard-control, and projectile-emission systems in the example project are game code, not built-in Engine services.</li></ul><p>Direct SDL access through <code>GetSDLRenderer()</code> is available for temporary game-owned solutions, but keep that code inside your game project.</p>`});
designerWiki.pages.push(
{id:'actors',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Actors: moving world objects',summary:'Create a world object that already has position and velocity data.',content:`<p>An Actor is a convenient type of entity for an object that exists in the game world and can move. Creating one gives it a TransformComponent (position, scale, and rotation) and a RigidBodyComponent (velocity).</p><pre><code>Actor::Config crateConfig;\ncrateConfig.position = Vector2(240, 160);\ncrateConfig.scale = Vector2(2, 2);\ncrateConfig.velocity = Vector2(0, 0);\nActor crate = world.CreateEntity&lt;Actor&gt;(crateConfig);</code></pre><p>Use Actor for invisible movers, triggers, basic props, or an object whose set of components you want to choose yourself. Add SpriteComponent if it should be drawn, BoxCollisionComponent if it should participate in box collision, and other components as the object needs them.</p><h2>Location helpers</h2><p><code>GetLocation()</code> reads the Actor’s current Transform position. <code>SetLocation(Vector2)</code> changes it directly. For ordinary frame-by-frame movement, set RigidBody velocity and let MovementSystem apply it during Update instead.</p>`},
{id:'characters',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Characters: ready-made game actors',summary:'Create a visible, moving, collidable actor with animation and health data.',content:`<p>Character is the best starting type for a player, enemy, or other on-screen character. It is an Actor, so it already has Transform and RigidBody. It also adds Sprite, BoxCollision, Animation, and Health components in one creation step.</p><pre><code>Character::Config playerConfig;\nplayerConfig.actor.position = Vector2(100, 100);\nplayerConfig.actor.velocity = Vector2(120, 0);\nplayerConfig.sprite.assetID = "player";\nplayerConfig.sprite.width = 32;\nplayerConfig.sprite.height = 32;\nplayerConfig.collision.width = 28;\nplayerConfig.collision.height = 28;\nplayerConfig.health.healthPercentage = 100;\nCharacter player = world.CreateEntity&lt;Character&gt;(playerConfig);</code></pre><h2>Choose Character when</h2><ul><li>The object needs a visible sprite and movement.</li><li>You expect it to take part in box collision.</li><li>It needs an animation component, even when it starts as a single-frame sprite.</li><li>Health percentage is useful to your game.</li></ul><p>Set <code>animation.numFrames</code>, <code>animation.frameSpeedRate</code>, and <code>animation.loops</code> for a horizontal spritesheet. The defaults describe one frame at one frame per second and loop enabled. Character does not add game rules such as player controls, attacks, damage, death, or AI; write those in your game project.</p>`},
{id:'first-playable',section:'Getting started',title:'First playable: a moving sprite',summary:'A complete small recipe for a texture, entity, movement, input, and rendering.',content:`<div class="notice">This recipe builds the smallest useful game loop using your project’s Game class and public Engine tools.</div><h2>1. Keep game state</h2><p>In <code>Game.h</code>, keep a context pointer so an input callback can request quit or change state:</p><pre><code>class Game final : public IGame {\npublic:\n  GameConfig Configure() const override;\n  void Start(GameContext&amp; context) override;\n  void BeginFrame(GameContext&amp; context) override;\n  void Update(GameContext&amp; context, float deltaSeconds) override;\n  void Render(GameContext&amp; context) override;\nprivate:\n  GameContext* context = nullptr;\n  bool paused = false;\n  void OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event);\n};</code></pre><h2>2. Load an image and create a player</h2><p>Put an image at the path used below. Add systems before creating entities. A moving sprite needs Transform, RigidBody, Sprite, and a texture whose ID matches the Sprite.</p><pre><code>void Game::Start(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  this-&gt;context = &amp;context;\n  auto&amp; world = context.GetWorld();\n  world.AddSystem&lt;RenderSystem&gt;();\n  world.AddSystem&lt;MovementSystem&gt;();\n  if (!context.GetAssets().AddTexture(context.GetSDLRenderer(),\n        "player", "assets/images/player.png")) {\n    context.RequestQuit(); return;\n  }\n  Entity player = world.CreateEntity();\n  player.AddComponent&lt;TransformComponent&gt;(Vector2(100, 100));\n  player.AddComponent&lt;RigidBodyComponent&gt;(Vector2(120, 0));\n  player.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;("player", 32, 32);\n}</code></pre><h2>3. Run and draw systems</h2><p>Engine calls your lifecycle functions, but it does not guess which game systems to run. Run movement during Update and sprites during Render:</p><pre><code>void Game::Update(GameContext&amp; context, float deltaSeconds) {\n  if (paused) return;\n  if (auto* movement = context.GetWorld().GetSystem&lt;MovementSystem&gt;())\n    movement-&gt;Update(deltaSeconds);\n}\nvoid Game::Render(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  if (auto* render = context.GetWorld().GetSystem&lt;RenderSystem&gt;())\n    render-&gt;Update(context.GetSDLRenderer(), context.GetAssets(),\n                   context.GetCamera());\n}</code></pre><h2>4. Add pause and quit</h2><pre><code>void Game::BeginFrame(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;KeyPressedEvent&gt;(\n    this, &amp;Game::OnKeyPressed);\n}\nvoid Game::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_P) paused = !paused;\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_ESCAPE) context-&gt;RequestQuit();\n}</code></pre><p>Include <code>&lt;SDL3/SDL.h&gt;</code> where you use SDL key constants. This pause stops the game logic behind it while Engine can continue receiving input and presenting frames.</p><p>Next, add animation, collision, or a scrolling camera from the pages below.</p>`},
{id:'systems',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Built-in systems',summary:'Register each system once, then call it from the correct game callback.',content:`<p>A system operates on every entity with the components it needs. Add it once in Start, then call its public Update function yourself.</p><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Call from</th><th>Entity needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RenderSystem</td><td>Render</td><td>Transform + Sprite</td></tr><tr><td>MovementSystem</td><td>Update</td><td>Transform + RigidBody</td></tr><tr><td>AnimationSystem</td><td>Update</td><td>Sprite + Animation</td></tr><tr><td>CollisionSystem</td><td>Update</td><td>Transform + BoxCollision</td></tr><tr><td>CameraMovementSystem</td><td>Update</td><td>Transform + CameraFollow</td></tr><tr><td>ProjectileLifecycleSystem</td><td>Update</td><td>Projectile</td></tr><tr><td>RenderCollisionSystem</td><td>Render</td><td>Transform + BoxCollision</td></tr></tbody></table><pre><code>world.AddSystem&lt;MovementSystem&gt;(); // once, in Start\nif (auto* movement = world.GetSystem&lt;MovementSystem&gt;())\n  movement-&gt;Update(deltaSeconds); // each Update</code></pre><p>CollisionSystem needs <code>collision-&gt;Update(context.GetEvents())</code>. CameraMovementSystem needs <code>camera-&gt;Update(context.GetCamera(), worldSize)</code>. RenderSystem needs renderer, assets, and camera as shown in the first-playable recipe. RenderCollisionSystem is an optional debugging helper that draws collision outlines.</p><div class="notice warning">Damage, shooting controls, and projectile spawning are not built-in systems. The example project owns those game rules, so implement equivalent systems in your own project when needed.</div>`},
{id:'collision',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Box collision and reactions',summary:'Detect axis-aligned rectangle overlaps and react through CollisionEvent.',content:`<p>CollisionSystem uses axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABBs): rectangular hit areas that do not rotate. Attach TransformComponent and BoxCollisionComponent, then update CollisionSystem each game frame.</p><pre><code>Entity wall = world.CreateEntity();\nwall.AddComponent&lt;TransformComponent&gt;(Vector2(320, 160));\nwall.AddComponent&lt;BoxCollisionComponent&gt;(64, 32);\n\nvoid Game::BeginFrame(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;CollisionEvent&gt;(\n    this, &amp;Game::OnCollision);\n}</code></pre><p>Call <code>collision-&gt;Update(context.GetEvents())</code> in Update after movement. Contacts are reported immediately and repeat every frame while two boxes overlap. Width and height use Transform scale; offsets move the box relative to its Transform position.</p><div class="notice warning">There are no circle colliders, radius checks, collision layers, masks, automatic physical resolution, or built-in damage response. Use boxes and author the game rule that follows a contact.</div>`},
{id:'camera',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Camera and world scrolling',summary:'Use the camera for a moving world and fixed sprites for screen-space art.',content:`<p>The camera starts at <code>cameraPosition</code> in GameConfig. RenderSystem subtracts its position from ordinary sprites, so the world scrolls as the camera moves. A SpriteComponent marked fixed is drawn in screen space instead and is useful for simple HUD images.</p><pre><code>world.AddSystem&lt;CameraMovementSystem&gt;();\n// During Update, after moving the followed entity:\ncameraSystem-&gt;Update(context.GetCamera(), Vector2(2000, 1200));</code></pre><p>CameraMovementSystem follows entities with TransformComponent and CameraFollowComponent, then keeps the view inside the world size you provide. Give the player a CameraFollowComponent and supply your actual level width and height. A single follow target is the clearest starting point.</p>`},
{id:'ui-audio',section:'Presentation',title:'HUD, text, menus, and audio',summary:'What you can present now, and what your game must currently provide itself.',content:`<h2>Simple visual overlays</h2><p>Use fixed sprites for image-based screen overlays such as a health frame or pause icon. The Engine does not provide a text, font, layout, button, menu, or general UI system yet.</p><p>For a temporary game-owned HUD panel, use the borrowed SDL renderer in Render:</p><pre><code>SDL_FRect panel{12, 12, 180, 36};\nSDL_SetRenderDrawColor(context.GetSDLRenderer(), 0, 0, 0, 180);\nSDL_RenderFillRect(context.GetSDLRenderer(), &amp;panel);</code></pre><p>This is lower-level SDL code. Keep it in your game project, draw it in Render, and set the draw color again before later custom shapes. Bring a game-owned bitmap font or text solution for labels until an Engine text API exists.</p><h2>Audio</h2><p>No game-facing Engine audio API is available yet. Plan sound effects and music as a game-owned integration; textures added through AssetManager do not become playable audio. Keep audio calls behind a small game-side helper so they are easy to replace when an Engine audio service arrives.</p>`}
);

// 2026-09-23: scene-oriented game workflow.
designerWiki.reviewed = '2026-09-23 CDT';
// The detailed audio article is defined later in this historical data file.
// Reserve its ID so the current source-derived override below applies safely.
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'tutorial-one-shot-audio',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Tutorial: play a one-shot sound effect',summary:'Scene-based one-shot audio pattern.',content:''});
designerWiki.groups.find(group => group.title === 'Your game').pages.splice(2, 0, 'scenes');
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'scenes',section:'Your game',title:'Scenes: separate levels and screens',summary:'Register named scenes, choose a starting scene, and change them safely.',content:`<p>A Scene is one self-contained game screen: a level, title screen, pause screen, or results screen. Each active scene gets its own World and event list. Changing scenes removes the old scene’s objects, subscriptions, and managed text together.</p><h2>Register the first scene</h2><pre><code>GameConfig Game::Configure() const { GameConfig config; config.startingScene = "title"; return config; }<br>void Game::RegisterScenes(SceneManager&amp; scenes) { scenes.Register&lt;TitleScene&gt;("title"); scenes.Register&lt;PlayScene&gt;("play"); }</code></pre><p>The starting ID must be registered, and scene types need a public default constructor.</p><h2>Put work in the right place</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Game::LoadContent</td><td>Assets and audio shared by scenes.</td></tr><tr><td>Scene::OnStart</td><td>Objects, HUD labels, event subscriptions, and custom systems for that scene.</td></tr><tr><td>Scene::Update</td><td>Rules only that scene needs.</td></tr><tr><td>Scene::OnEnd</td><td>Optional final work while its World still exists.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Change scene</h2><pre><code>context.GetScenes().GoTo("play");<br>context.GetScenes().Restart(); // reload current scene</code></pre><p>GoTo returns false for an unknown ID, no active scene, or an existing request. A valid request is applied at the end-of-update boundary. The old scene receives OnEnd, then its World, EventManager, and labels clear; a fresh World starts the new scene. Do not keep World or event references across a transition.</p><h2>Callbacks</h2><pre><code>OnStart → each frame: BeginFrame → Update → Render → OnEnd</code></pre><p>Engine standard systems run before Scene::Update and draw sprites before Scene::Render. Game-level frame callbacks remain available for behavior that truly spans scenes.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'lifecycle'), {content:`<pre><code>Configure → RegisterScenes → LoadContent → first Scene::OnStart<br>each frame: Game::BeginFrame → Scene::BeginFrame → input → Engine systems<br>→ Scene::Update → Game::Update → World changes → scene transition<br>→ clear → Engine sprites → Scene::Render → Game::Render → text → present<br>active Scene::OnEnd → Game::Stop → Engine cleanup</code></pre><p>Configure runs before Engine services exist. RegisterScenes registers factories, LoadContent loads game-wide content, and the selected scene then starts. Put level objects and scene-only subscriptions in Scene::OnStart.</p><p>Transitions happen after World changes, never in the middle of a callback. The old scene’s OnEnd runs while its World exists; then the World, EventManager, and managed labels clear before the next scene starts. Game::Stop runs only when the whole application exits.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'igame'), {content:`<p>Your <code>Game</code> derives from IGame. All callbacks have empty defaults.</p><pre><code>GameConfig Configure() const override;<br>void RegisterScenes(SceneManager&amp; scenes) override;<br>void LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp; audio) override;<br>void Stop() override;<br>void BeginFrame(GameContext&amp; context) override;<br>void Update(GameContext&amp; context, float deltaSeconds) override;<br>void Render(GameContext&amp; context) override;</code></pre><p>Use Configure for settings and startingScene; RegisterScenes for each scene type; LoadContent for shared asset/audio loading; and scenes for gameplay. Stop is whole-game cleanup and has no context. GetContext is protected and valid only while Application runs.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'config'), {content:`<p>Return GameConfig from Configure. <code>startingScene</code> is required and must name a scene registered in RegisterScenes.</p><pre><code>GameConfig Game::Configure() const {<br>  GameConfig config; config.title = "My Gobo Game";<br>  config.startingScene = "title";<br>  config.window.windowWidth = 1280; config.window.windowHeight = 720;<br>  config.render.renderWidth = 1280; config.render.renderHeight = 720;<br>  return config;<br>}</code></pre><p>Defaults include a 1920×1080 window, a 1280×720 logical view, camera (0,0), VSync, a 60 FPS cap, and clear color (21,21,21,255). targetFPS must be greater than zero. SystemsConfig applies to every fresh scene World.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'context'), {content:`<p>GameContext provides <code>GetWorld()</code>, <code>GetEvents()</code>, <code>GetScenes()</code>, <code>GetAssets()</code>, <code>GetAudio()</code>, <code>GetText()</code>, <code>GetCamera()</code>, <code>GetViewSize()</code>, <code>FindObject(name)</code>, and <code>RequestQuit()</code>.</p><p>World and Events belong to the active scene: use them in scene callbacks and never keep their pointers across a transition. Assets and Audio are game-wide. Text labels clear during a scene change. GetScenes requests a transition; RequestQuit exits the application.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'first-playable'), {summary:'Create a scene, place a Character in it, and let configured Engine systems run it.',content:`<div class="notice">A playable object belongs in a Scene so it does not leak into later levels or screens.</div><h2>1. Register a playable scene</h2><pre><code>GameConfig Game::Configure() const { GameConfig config; config.startingScene = "play"; return config; }<br>void Game::RegisterScenes(SceneManager&amp; scenes) { scenes.Register&lt;PlayScene&gt;("play"); }<br>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp;) { assets.AddTexture("player", "assets/images/player.png"); }</code></pre><h2>2. Create the Character in OnStart</h2><pre><code>void PlayScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) {<br>  Character::Config player; player.actor.position = Vector2(100, 100);<br>  player.sprite.assetID = "player"; player.sprite.width = player.sprite.height = 32;<br>  player.collision.width = player.collision.height = 28;<br>  context.GetWorld().CreateCharacter&lt;Character&gt;("player", player);<br>}</code></pre><p>Character provides Transform, RigidBody, Sprite, BoxCollision, Animation, and Health. Standard movement and rendering are enabled by default. For a real player, use a Character subclass as shown in the Character tutorial.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'tutorial-one-shot-audio'), {content:`<p>This recipe plays a one-shot sound from a Scene. It does not provide audio assets: use a sound file you own, for example <code>assets/sounds/confirm.wav</code>.</p><h2>Load shared audio once</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp;, AudioManager&amp; audio) { audio.LoadAudio("ui.confirm", "assets/sounds/confirm.wav", true); }</code></pre><h2>Subscribe in the scene</h2><pre><code>void TitleScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) { context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;KeyPressedEvent&gt;(this, &amp;TitleScene::OnKeyPressed); }</code></pre><h2>Play and change screen</h2><pre><code>void TitleScene::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {<br>  if (event.symbol != SDLK_RETURN) return;<br>  GetContext()-&gt;GetAudio().PlaySFX("ui.confirm", 0.75f);<br>  GetContext()-&gt;GetScenes().GoTo("play");<br>}</code></pre><p>The subscription disappears with the scene EventManager on transition. AudioManager is Engine-owned; do not initialize or shut it down. This is a code pattern only and does not add a sound pack.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'assets'), {content:`<h2>Textures</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp;) {<br>  assets.AddTexture("hero", "assets/images/hero.png");<br>}</code></pre><p>Load shared textures before the first scene starts. AddTexture returns false for a duplicate ID or loading failure. A scene uses that ID in its SpriteComponent. AssetManager owns the renderer dependency, so game code supplies only an ID and file path.</p><h2>Fonts</h2><p>Use AddFont(id, filepath, pointSize) for a game font and reference its ID in TextLabelConfig. The embedded <code>gobo.font.default</code> is ready for ordinary labels and cannot be removed or replaced.</p>`});
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'audio',section:'Presentation',title:'Sound and music',summary:'Scene-based playback through Engine-managed audio.',content:''});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'audio'), {content:`<p>Application creates, updates, and shuts down AudioManager. Use the AudioManager passed to Game::LoadContent for shared audio assets; access context.GetAudio inside a Scene when playback depends on scene actions.</p><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp;, AudioManager&amp; audio) {<br>  audio.LoadAudio("theme", "assets/sounds/theme.wav");<br>  audio.SetBusVolume("Music", 0.5f);<br>}<br>void TitleScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) {<br>  context.GetAudio().PlayMusic("theme", true);<br>}</code></pre><p>LoadAudio associates an ID with a file. Play returns an AudioPlaybackID; PlaySFX, PlayMusic, PlayDialogue, and PlayCustom1–3 select conventional buses. The general Play call takes a bus, volume, and loop count. Do not create or shut down another AudioManager.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'input'), {title:'Keyboard input',summary:'Give an object keyboard callbacks, or subscribe a scene to an event.',content:`<p>Keyboard input has two useful patterns. For a player, enemy, or other individual object, add <code>KeyboardControlledComponent</code> in OnCreated and override its callbacks. For a scene-wide action such as opening a pause menu, subscribe the Scene to the event in OnStart.</p><h2>Object controls</h2><pre><code>void OnCreated() override {<br>  AddComponent&lt;KeyboardControlledComponent&gt;();<br>}<br>void OnAllKeys(KeyAllEvent&amp; event) override {<br>  Vector2 velocity(0);<br>  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_LEFT)) velocity.x -= 200;<br>  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_RIGHT)) velocity.x += 200;<br>  GetRigidBody()-&gt;velocity = velocity;<br>}</code></pre><p>KeyboardControlSystem is enabled by default. It forwards <code>OnKeyPressed</code> once on the initial press, <code>OnKeyHeld</code> every frame for each held key, <code>OnKeyReleased</code> when it goes up, and <code>OnAllKeys</code> once per frame with every held key. OnAllKeys is the clearest way to combine directions.</p><h2>Scene-wide action</h2><pre><code>void PlayScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) {<br>  context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;KeyPressedEvent&gt;(<br>    this, &amp;PlayScene::OnKeyPressed);<br>}<br>void PlayScene::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {<br>  if (event.symbol == SDLK_ESCAPE) GetContext()-&gt;GetScenes().GoTo("pause");<br>}</code></pre><p>Event subscriptions remain until the owner unsubscribes or the scene changes. Never retain a subscription to an object you destroy.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'systems'), {content:`<p>GameConfig.systems chooses standard systems. Each fresh scene World receives the enabled systems automatically. Leave a system enabled unless your game deliberately provides a replacement.</p><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>What it does</th><th>Needed components</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rendering</td><td>Draws sprites before Scene::Render</td><td>Transform + Sprite</td></tr><tr><td>Movement</td><td>Applies velocity</td><td>Transform + RigidBody</td></tr><tr><td>Animation</td><td>Advances sprite frames</td><td>Sprite + Animation</td></tr><tr><td>Collision</td><td>Reports box overlaps</td><td>Transform + BoxCollision</td></tr><tr><td>Keyboard control</td><td>Forwards keyboard events to objects</td><td>KeyboardControlled</td></tr><tr><td>Mouse control</td><td>Forwards button events to objects</td><td>MouseControlled</td></tr><tr><td>Mouse collision</td><td>Calls click hooks when the pointer is inside an object’s box</td><td>MouseControlled + Transform + BoxCollision</td></tr><tr><td>Camera movement</td><td>Follows eligible world objects</td><td>Transform + CameraFollow</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Projectile lifetime and collision-debug rendering are also available. Custom game rules remain game code: add your custom system inside Scene::OnStart and update it in Scene::Update.</p>`});
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'objects',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Named game objects and callbacks',summary:'Object lifecycle and behavior hooks.',content:''});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'objects'), {content:`<p>Actor and Character are named EntityObject types. They combine an Entity handle with helpful component access and callbacks. Create one through the active scene World and only keep its reference while it is alive.</p><pre><code>Player&amp; player = context.GetWorld().CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player");<br>EntityObject* found = context.FindObject("player");</code></pre><h2>Useful callbacks</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Callback</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OnCreated</td><td>Add components and apply your object configuration.</td></tr><tr><td>Update(float)</td><td>Per-object behavior; set <code>Updates = false</code> to opt out.</td></tr><tr><td>OnCollision</td><td>React to an overlapping box.</td></tr><tr><td>Keyboard hooks</td><td>Controls for one object.</td></tr><tr><td>Mouse button hooks</td><td>Pointer actions anywhere in the playable view.</td></tr><tr><td>OnLeftClicked / OnRightClicked</td><td>Pointer clicks that also hit this object’s box.</td></tr><tr><td>OnDestroyed</td><td>Local cleanup before the object disappears.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Names are unique and nonempty. Tags are unique labels; groups collect many entities. Each object currently has one tag and one group, so set them deliberately.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'limits'), {content:`<h2>Ready to use</h2><ul><li>Scenes, named Actor/Character objects, object callbacks, sprite rendering, animation, movement, box collision, keyboard and mouse events, managed text, and managed audio.</li><li>Screen-space fixed sprites and text; world-space pointer coordinates that follow the camera.</li><li>Standard systems selected through GameConfig.</li></ul><h2>Current boundaries</h2><ul><li>Collision is rectangle-only: no circles, layers/masks, physical pushback, or automatic damage.</li><li>Text labels are simple managed labels; there is no full layout, button, or menu toolkit.</li><li>Projectile spawning, combat, AI, and game rules belong in your project.</li><li>Tags are unique and groups may contain many entities, but an entity currently has one of each.</li></ul><p>Gobo Engine is actively evolving. Treat ExampleGame as a learning reference, not a gameplay package to depend on.</p>`});
designerWiki.pages.push(
{id:'events-guide',section:'Input & interaction',title:'Events: react at the right level',summary:'Choose scene subscriptions, object callbacks, or click hooks.',content:`<p>An event is an immediate notification. Use a Scene subscription for a screen-wide rule, object callbacks for one object’s behavior, and click hooks when a button-like object must also be under the pointer.</p><h2>Event timing and safety</h2><p>EventManager calls listeners immediately. A listener added while an event is being sent waits until a later event of that type. Remove an object’s subscriptions before it is destroyed; changing scenes removes the entire scene EventManager.</p><h2>Keyboard events</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>KeyPressedEvent</td><td>First key-down only.</td></tr><tr><td>KeyHeldEvent</td><td>One event per held key per frame.</td></tr><tr><td>KeyReleasedEvent</td><td>Key-up, including focus-loss cleanup.</td></tr><tr><td>KeyAllEvent</td><td>One per frame containing all held keys.</td></tr><tr><td>SceneStartedEvent</td><td>Emitted immediately after a scene’s OnStart succeeds.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Pointer events</h2><p>There are pressed, held, and released events for both left and right buttons. Each inherits MousePositionEvent with <code>mouseLocation</code> in world coordinates and <code>screenLocation</code> in logical render-view coordinates. Check <code>valid</code> and <code>insideView</code> before treating a press as a game action. Releases may arrive outside the view or with <code>cancelled</code> true after focus loss; use them to clear drag state rather than trigger a click.</p><div class="notice">Pointer presses are accepted only inside the logical game view. This prevents letterbox bars or other window space from affecting gameplay.</div>`},
{id:'mouse-input',section:'Input & interaction',title:'Mouse input and clickable objects',summary:'Build world clicks, screen buttons, dragging, and hit-tested interactions.',content:`<p>Add <code>MouseControlledComponent</code> to an Actor or Character to receive left/right pressed, held, and released callbacks. Keep <code>useMouseControlSystem</code> enabled (the default). Include <code>&lt;Components/MouseControlledComponent.h&gt;</code> in a game file until it is included by your chosen project header set.</p><h2>World click or drag</h2><pre><code>void OnCreated() override { AddComponent&lt;MouseControlledComponent&gt;(); }<br>void OnLeftMouseButtonPressed(LeftMouseButtonPressedEvent&amp; event) override {<br>  if (!event.valid || !event.insideView) return;<br>  SetLocation(event.mouseLocation);<br>}<br>void OnLeftMouseButtonHeld(LeftMouseButtonHeldEvent&amp; event) override {<br>  if (!event.cancelled) SetLocation(event.mouseLocation);<br>}</code></pre><p><code>mouseLocation</code> already includes camera position, so use it for world objects. Use <code>screenLocation</code> for fixed HUD art. A held sequence ends with a release; clear drag state on every release, including cancelled ones.</p><h2>Clickable object</h2><pre><code>void OnCreated() override {<br>  AddComponent&lt;MouseControlledComponent&gt;();<br>  AddComponent&lt;BoxCollisionComponent&gt;(160, 48);<br>}<br>void OnLeftClicked() override {<br>  // Start a level, open a dialog, or request a scene change.<br>}</code></pre><p>MouseCollisionSystem is enabled by default. It requires MouseControlled, Transform, and BoxCollision. It calls OnLeftClicked or OnRightClicked only for a press inside the object’s scaled box. A fixed sprite is tested against screenLocation; a normal sprite is tested against mouseLocation.</p><h2>Button checklist</h2><ol><li>Use an Actor or Character with Transform.</li><li>Add MouseControlledComponent and BoxCollisionComponent in OnCreated.</li><li>Use a fixed SpriteComponent for a HUD/menu button.</li><li>Override OnLeftClicked for a simple press, or button-event hooks for dragging/press state.</li></ol>`}
);
// 2026-09-26: automatic sprite import and Sprite/Animation definitions.
designerWiki.reviewed = '2026-09-26 CDT';
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'assets'), {title:'Assets and automatic sprites',summary:'Put PNG artwork in Assets/Sprites and use its imported Sprite ID.',content:`<p>Every game starts by importing PNG files under <code>Assets/Sprites</code>. You do not call ImportSprites yourself. The build stages the project’s <code>Assets</code> folder beside the game executable, then Engine imports every PNG in the Sprites folder before Game::LoadContent.</p><h2>Imported IDs</h2><p>The ID is the path relative to <code>Assets/Sprites</code>, without <code>.png</code>. For example, <code>Assets/Sprites/player.png</code> becomes <code>"player"</code>, while <code>Assets/Sprites/ui/icons/heart.png</code> becomes <code>"ui/icons/heart"</code>. Only PNG files import; duplicate IDs or a failed image stop startup.</p><h2>Use an imported static sprite</h2><pre><code>void PlayScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) {<br>  auto hero = context.GetAssets().GetSprite("player");<br>  Entity sprite = context.GetWorld().CreateAutoNamedEntity("hero");<br>  sprite.AddComponent&lt;TransformComponent&gt;(Vector2(120, 90));<br>  sprite.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;(hero, 1);<br>}</code></pre><p>GetSprite returns a shared definition. Check it before using an optional asset. Use <code>GetAssetPath("sounds/confirm.wav")</code> when an API needs a real file path inside Assets, such as AudioManager or map-file loading.</p><h2>Custom definitions</h2><p><code>DefineSprite(id, Sprite(...))</code> makes a cropped region from an imported texture. It is useful for tile atlases and animations. DefineSprite checks that the requested region fits its imported PNG.</p><h2>Fonts</h2><p>Use AddFont for a game font and reference it from TextLabelConfig. <code>gobo.font.default</code> is built in for ordinary labels.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'characters'), {content:`<p>Character extends Actor with SpriteComponent, BoxCollisionComponent, and HealthComponent. Its visual comes from a <code>SpriteRef</code>, usually from AssetManager::GetSprite.</p><pre><code>Character::Config hero;<br>hero.actor.position = Vector2(100, 100);<br>hero.actor.scale = Vector2(2);<br>hero.collision = {32, 32};<br>hero.sprite.asset = context.GetAssets().GetSprite("player");<br>hero.sprite.layer = 1;<br>world.CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player", hero);</code></pre><p>Character does not automatically add controls, attacks, AI, or game rules. Add those in an object callback or scene-owned system. Use GetSprite, GetBoxCollision, and GetHealth to access the supplied components.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'first-playable'), {summary:'Create an imported-sprite Character in a scene.',content:`<div class="notice">Place <code>player.png</code> in your project’s <code>Assets/Sprites</code> folder before building. Engine imports it as <code>"player"</code> before LoadContent.</div><h2>1. Register the scene</h2><pre><code>GameConfig Game::Configure() const { GameConfig c; c.startingScene = "play"; return c; }<br>void Game::RegisterScenes(SceneManager&amp; scenes) { scenes.Register&lt;PlayScene&gt;("play"); }</code></pre><h2>2. Create a Character from the imported Sprite</h2><pre><code>void PlayScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) {<br>  auto playerSprite = context.GetAssets().GetSprite("player");<br>  if (!playerSprite) { context.RequestQuit(); return; }<br>  Character::Config player;<br>  player.actor.position = Vector2(100, 100);<br>  player.sprite.asset = playerSprite;<br>  player.collision = {32, 32};<br>  context.GetWorld().CreateCharacter&lt;Character&gt;("player", player);<br>}</code></pre><p>Rendering and movement systems are on by default. A static imported image is already a Sprite definition; use an Animation definition when the PNG is a sheet.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'scenes'), {content:`<p>A Scene is one self-contained game screen. Its World, event listeners, objects, and managed text disappear together when you change scene.</p><h2>Register and start</h2><pre><code>GameConfig Game::Configure() const { GameConfig c; c.startingScene = "title"; return c; }<br>void Game::RegisterScenes(SceneManager&amp; scenes) {<br>  scenes.Register&lt;TitleScene&gt;("title");<br>  scenes.Register&lt;PlayScene&gt;("play");<br>}</code></pre><h2>Scene work</h2><p>Use Game::LoadContent to define shared sprites and load shared audio. Use Scene::OnStart for objects, labels, subscriptions, and custom systems. Scene::Update owns level-specific rules; OnEnd is the final safe place to inspect the old World.</p><h2>View size</h2><pre><code>if (!SetViewSize(context, 1280, 720)) return;</code></pre><p>Scene::SetViewSize updates the logical view and camera together. It requests quit and returns false if the requested dimensions are too small or cannot be applied.</p><h2>Transitions</h2><pre><code>context.GetScenes().GoTo("play");<br>context.GetScenes().Restart();</code></pre><p>A request applies after update work completes. The old scene gets OnEnd; then its World, EventManager, and labels clear before the next scene receives OnStart.</p>`});
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'sprites-animation',section:'Artwork & animation',title:'Sprites, animation, and layers',summary:'Turn imported artwork into static sprites, tile regions, or named animation rows.',content:`<p>A Sprite is an immutable definition: texture ID, frame size, and optional origin. SpriteComponent stores which definition an object uses and its current playback, visibility, layer, alpha, fixed-screen, and flip settings.</p><h2>Static art</h2><pre><code>auto icon = context.GetAssets().GetSprite("ui/icons/heart");<br>actor.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;(icon, 20, 255, true);</code></pre><p>The constructor arguments after the Sprite are layer, alpha (0–255), fixed-screen flag, and hidden flag. Lower layers draw first. Layers are clamped to −99 through 99. A fixed sprite ignores camera movement and is suited to HUD art.</p><h2>Animation sheet</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp;) {<br>  Animation hero("hero-sheet", 32, 32, 12.0f, 4, 4);<br>  hero.SetRow(0, "up"); hero.SetRow(1, "right");<br>  hero.SetRow(2, "down"); hero.SetRow(3, "left");<br>  assets.DefineSprite("hero", hero);<br>}<br>// Later, after assigning assets.GetSprite("hero"):<br>sprite-&gt;Play("right");</code></pre><p>The sheet must be imported as <code>hero-sheet</code>. Animation arguments are frame width/height, default frames per second, default frames per row, and row count. Row IDs are case-insensitive and must be unique. DefineSprite rejects a definition that extends outside its texture.</p><h2>Playback controls</h2><p>Play(row, loop, restart) chooses a row. Pause, Resume, Stop, Restart, SetFPS, IsPlaying, IsFinished, GetCurrentFrame, and GetAnimationID manage or inspect playback. AnimationSystem advances SpriteComponents automatically each frame. SetFlip(horizontal, vertical), SetAlpha, SetLayer, SetFixed, and SetIsHidden control presentation.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'template'), {content:`<p>Start every game by copying the supplied template with <code>.\\New-GoboProject.ps1 -Name MyGame</code>. Its CMake setup links your project to Gobo Engine and stages your game’s <code>Assets</code> folder beside the executable.</p><h2>Where files belong</h2><ul><li><code>Assets/Sprites</code>: PNG artwork that Engine imports automatically.</li><li><code>Assets/sounds</code>, <code>Assets/tilemaps</code>, and similar folders: game files resolved with AssetManager::GetAssetPath.</li><li><code>src</code>: your Game, Scene, object, and game-rule code.</li></ul><p>Do not edit the Engine directory to add one game’s content. Keep all artwork and rules in the copied project so it can build, run, and package independently.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'tutorial-one-shot-audio'), {title:'Tutorial: play a one-shot sound effect',summary:'Load a game-owned sound once and trigger it from a scene action.',content:`<p>Place a sound file you own under <code>Assets</code>, such as <code>Assets/sounds/confirm.wav</code>. This pattern does not add or generate an audio asset.</p><h2>Load it once</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp; audio) {<br>  audio.LoadAudio("ui.confirm", assets.GetAssetPath("sounds/confirm.wav"), true);<br>}</code></pre><h2>Play it from a scene</h2><pre><code>void TitleScene::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {<br>  if (event.symbol == SDLK_RETURN)<br>    GetContext()-&gt;GetAudio().PlaySFX("ui.confirm", 0.75f);<br>}</code></pre><p>AudioManager is Engine-owned: do not initialize, update, or shut it down yourself. Check the returned playback ID only when later pause, resume, or stop control is needed.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'audio'), {content:`<p>Application creates, updates, and shuts down AudioManager. Load shared audio in Game::LoadContent, then use context.GetAudio from a Scene when playback belongs to a scene action.</p><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp; audio) {<br>  audio.LoadAudio("theme", assets.GetAssetPath("sounds/theme.wav"));<br>  audio.SetBusVolume("Music", 0.5f);<br>}<br>void TitleScene::OnStart(GameContext&amp; context) {<br>  context.GetAudio().PlayMusic("theme", true);<br>}</code></pre><p>Put the file under <code>Assets/sounds</code>. Play returns an AudioPlaybackID; PlaySFX, PlayMusic, PlayDialogue, and PlayCustom1–3 choose conventional buses. Do not create or shut down another AudioManager.</p>`});
const sceneDocumentationOverrides = Object.fromEntries(['template','lifecycle','igame','config','context','first-playable','tutorial-one-shot-audio','assets','audio','input','systems','objects','limits','characters','scenes'].map(id => {
  const page = designerWiki.pages.find(candidate => candidate.id === id);
  return [id, {content: page.content, summary: page.summary, title: page.title}];
}));
const spriteTutorialOverrides = {
  'examplegame-tour': {title:'Read ExampleGame as a blueprint',summary:'Use the shipped project to see scenes, imported sprites, tiles, sound, and characters working together.',content:`<p><code>Projects/ExampleGame</code> is a read-only learning reference. Its Game registers a scene, creates animation and tile definitions in LoadContent, and lets the scene create the world.</p><table><thead><tr><th>ExampleGame part</th><th>Pattern to reuse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ExampleGame.cpp::LoadContent</td><td>Turn imported PNGs into named Animation and cropped Sprite definitions; load sound with GetAssetPath.</td></tr><tr><td>Scenes/ExampleScene.cpp::OnStart</td><td>Set the view, add a game-owned system, create tiles, objects, and HUD.</td></tr><tr><td>Entities/player.h</td><td>A Character subclass that selects an animation row while responding to held keys.</td></tr><tr><td>Entities/radar.h</td><td>A fixed-screen Actor sprite used as simple HUD art.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Copy a pattern into your own project, then use your own IDs, artwork, level data, and game rules.</p>`},
  'tutorial-player': {title:'Tutorial: a controllable animated Character',summary:'Create a Character from an imported animation, then choose rows from held-key input.',content:`<h2>1. Define the animation once</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp;) {<br>  Animation hero("hero-sheet", 32, 32, 12.0f, 4, 4);<br>  hero.SetRow(0, "up"); hero.SetRow(1, "right");<br>  hero.SetRow(2, "down"); hero.SetRow(3, "left");<br>  assets.DefineSprite("hero", hero);<br>}</code></pre><p>This expects <code>Assets/Sprites/hero-sheet.png</code>. Engine imports that PNG before LoadContent.</p><h2>2. Create a Player</h2><pre><code>Character::Config player{<br>  .actor = {.position = Vector2(100, 100)},<br>  .collision = {32, 32},<br>  .sprite = {.asset = assets.GetSprite("hero"), .layer = 1}<br>};<br>world.CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player", player);</code></pre><h2>3. Move and select an animation row</h2><pre><code>void Player::OnAllKeys(KeyAllEvent&amp; event) {<br>  Vector2 velocity(0); auto* sprite = GetSprite();<br>  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_RIGHT)) { velocity.x = 200; sprite-&gt;Play("right"); }<br>  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_LEFT)) { velocity.x = -200; sprite-&gt;Play("left"); }<br>  GetRigidBody()-&gt;velocity = velocity;<br>}</code></pre><p>Use OnAllKeys when multiple directions may be held. Character supplies Transform, RigidBody, Sprite, collision, and health; controls remain your game’s code.</p>`},
  'tutorial-world': {title:'Tutorial: build a scrolling tile world',summary:'Crop tiles from an imported sheet and place them beneath characters.',content:`<h2>1. Define tile regions</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp;) {<br>  assets.DefineSprite("jungle.grass", Sprite("tilemaps/jungle", 32, 32, 0, 0));<br>  assets.DefineSprite("jungle.path", Sprite("tilemaps/jungle", 32, 32, 32, 0));<br>}</code></pre><p><code>tilemaps/jungle</code> is imported from <code>Assets/Sprites/tilemaps/jungle.png</code>. The final four values choose the top-left pixel of a 32×32 tile.</p><h2>2. Place an imported tile</h2><pre><code>auto tile = world.CreateAutoNamedEntity("tile");<br>tile.AddComponent&lt;TransformComponent&gt;(Vector2(x * 32, y * 32));<br>tile.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;(assets.GetSprite("jungle.grass"), -1);</code></pre><p>Use lower layers for terrain and higher layers for characters. Set your level’s world size and add CameraFollowComponent to the player for scrolling.</p><h2>3. Add fixed art</h2><pre><code>radar.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;(assets.GetSprite("radar"), 2, 255, true);</code></pre><p>The final true makes this sprite fixed to the screen instead of the world.</p>`},
  'tutorial-hud-audio': {title:'Tutorial: a health HUD with sound',summary:'Use TextManager for a label and AssetManager paths for game-owned sound.',content:`<h2>1. Create managed text</h2><pre><code>TextLabelConfig health{.text = "Health: 100", .position = Vector2(20, 20), .layer = 100};<br>context.GetText().Create("hud.health", health);</code></pre><p>TextManager owns the label, renders it automatically, uses <code>gobo.font.default</code> when no fontID is supplied, and clears it during scene shutdown. Do not construct, retain, render, or shut down a TextLabel yourself.</p><h2>2. Load and play sound</h2><pre><code>void Game::LoadContent(AssetManager&amp; assets, AudioManager&amp; audio) {<br>  audio.LoadAudio("helicopter", assets.GetAssetPath("sounds/helicopter.wav"), true);<br>}<br>context.GetAudio().PlaySFX("helicopter", 0.75f);</code></pre><p>Put the file under Assets, not Engine. AudioManager is Engine-owned and is updated and shut down by Engine.</p>`}
};
// One-shot audio recipe for a project created from GoboGameTemplate.
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'tutorial-one-shot-audio',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Tutorial: play a one-shot sound effect',summary:'Load one game-owned sound and trigger it once from a template game action.',content:`<p>This recipe shows the smallest one-shot sound effect pattern for a project copied from GoboGameTemplate. It does not provide an audio file: place a sound file you own in your game project, for example <code>assets/sounds/confirm.wav</code>.</p><h2>1. Add a callback declaration to Game.h</h2><pre><code>private:\n  void OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event);</code></pre><h2>2. Load once and subscribe once in Start</h2><p>Use a unique ID. EventManager subscriptions persist for the run, so register this callback in Start rather than once per frame.</p><pre><code>void Game::Start(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  context.GetAudio().LoadAudio(\n    "ui.confirm", "assets/sounds/confirm.wav", true);\n  context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;KeyPressedEvent&gt;(\n    this, &amp;Game::OnKeyPressed);\n}</code></pre><h2>3. Play the sound for the action</h2><pre><code>void Game::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {\n  if (event.symbol != SDLK_RETURN) return;\n  if (auto* context = GetContext()) {\n    const AudioPlaybackID playback =\n      context-&gt;GetAudio().PlaySFX("ui.confirm", 0.75f);\n    if (playback == InvalidAudioPlaybackID) {\n      // Loading or playback failed; keep the game usable.\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre><p><code>PlaySFX</code> plays once and returns an ID only if it started successfully. Call Stop, Pause, or Resume with that ID only when your game needs later control. The audio manager is Engine-owned and updated automatically; do not initialize or shut it down yourself.</p><div class="notice">Use this as a code pattern only. It intentionally does not add a sound asset, generate audio, or create an Engine-wide sound pack.</div>`});
// 2026-09-22 review overrides: Application-owned input, text, and audio APIs.
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'context'), {content:`<p>GameContext provides <code>GetWorld()</code>, <code>GetAssets()</code>, <code>GetEvents()</code>, <code>GetAudio()</code>, <code>GetCamera()</code>, <code>GetRenderer()</code>, <code>GetSDLRenderer()</code>, <code>FindObject(name)</code>, and <code>RequestQuit()</code>.</p><p>Use World to create and find named objects; Assets to load textures and fonts; Audio to load and play game sound; Camera to change the view; and RequestQuit to end cleanly. These references belong to Engine and are valid only while the game is running.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'limits'), {content:`<h2>What is ready</h2><ul><li>Named Actor and Character objects, object callbacks, texture-backed sprites, 2D movement, camera movement, animation, box collision, keyboard input, basic text labels, and managed audio are available.</li><li>Standard systems are selected in GameConfig and run automatically.</li><li>Vector2 is the normal 2D value type. Division by zero is guarded, but out-of-range vector indexing remains unsafe in release builds.</li></ul><h2>Plan around these boundaries</h2><ul><li>TextLabel is a low-level label primitive; there is no full UI layout, menu, or button system.</li><li>Collision is rectangle-only. There are no circles, radius queries, collision layers/masks, physical pushback, or built-in damage rules.</li><li>Tags are unique and groups can contain many objects, but each object currently has one of each.</li><li>Projectile spawning and combat rules remain game-owned. ExampleGame is a reference, not a drop-in gameplay package.</li></ul>`});

Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'first-playable'), {content:`<div class="notice">This recipe creates a playable Character: the ready-made Engine type for a visible, moving, collidable actor.</div><h2>1. Keep game state</h2><p>In <code>Game.h</code>, keep a context pointer for event callbacks and a pause flag:</p><pre><code>private:\n  GameContext* context = nullptr;\n  bool paused = false;\n  void OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event);</code></pre><h2>2. Load the player image and create a Character</h2><p>Add the systems first. Character supplies Transform, RigidBody, Sprite, BoxCollision, Animation, and Health components automatically, so you configure it instead of adding those components one by one.</p><pre><code>void Game::Start(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  this-&gt;context = &amp;context;\n  auto&amp; world = context.GetWorld();\n  world.AddSystem&lt;RenderSystem&gt;();\n  world.AddSystem&lt;MovementSystem&gt;();\n  if (!context.GetAssets().AddTexture(context.GetSDLRenderer(),\n        "player", "assets/images/player.png")) {\n    context.RequestQuit(); return;\n  }\n\n  Character::Config playerConfig;\n  playerConfig.actor.position = Vector2(100, 100);\n  playerConfig.actor.velocity = Vector2(120, 0);\n  playerConfig.sprite.assetID = "player";\n  playerConfig.sprite.width = 32;\n  playerConfig.sprite.height = 32;\n  playerConfig.collision.width = 28;\n  playerConfig.collision.height = 28;\n  Character player = world.CreateEntity&lt;Character&gt;(playerConfig);\n}</code></pre><p>The configured texture ID must match the ID passed to AddTexture. Character begins with a one-frame looping animation, which is fine for a still image. Set its animation fields later when you switch to a spritesheet.</p><h2>3. Run movement and draw the player</h2><p>Engine calls your lifecycle functions, but your project chooses which systems run:</p><pre><code>void Game::Update(GameContext&amp; context, float deltaSeconds) {\n  if (paused) return;\n  if (auto* movement = context.GetWorld().GetSystem&lt;MovementSystem&gt;())\n    movement-&gt;Update(deltaSeconds);\n}\nvoid Game::Render(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  if (auto* render = context.GetWorld().GetSystem&lt;RenderSystem&gt;())\n    render-&gt;Update(context.GetSDLRenderer(), context.GetAssets(),\n                   context.GetCamera());\n}</code></pre><h2>4. Add pause and quit</h2><pre><code>void Game::BeginFrame(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  context.GetEvents().SubscribeToEvent&lt;KeyPressedEvent&gt;(\n    this, &amp;Game::OnKeyPressed);\n}\nvoid Game::OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) {\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_P) paused = !paused;\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_ESCAPE) context-&gt;RequestQuit();\n}</code></pre><p>Include <code>&lt;SDL3/SDL.h&gt;</code> where you use SDL key constants. The Character page explains the supplied components and the gameplay rules you still need to add yourself.</p>`});

// The current workflow is configuration-driven and uses named EntityObject subclasses.
designerWiki.groups[2] = {title:'Gameplay tools',pages:['objects','entities','actors','characters','systems','assets','input','collision','camera']};
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'home'), {content:`<div class="notice">This guide is for people making a game project with Gobo Engine. Start from <code>Projects/GoboGameTemplate</code>; do not modify Engine source for ordinary game work.</div><h2>The current game-making model</h2><ol><li>Choose the built-in systems your game needs in <code>GameConfig</code>.</li><li>In Start, load assets, set the world size, and create <strong>named</strong> Actors or Characters.</li><li>Make a small class for each kind of game object. Put its setup in <code>OnCreated</code> and its reactions in the provided callbacks.</li><li>Use Update only for your game’s extra rules and custom systems. Engine runs enabled core systems for you.</li></ol><p>ExampleGame demonstrates this approach with Player, Enemy, Tank, Truck, and Radar classes.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'lifecycle'), {content:`<pre><code>Configure → Engine prepares selected systems → Start\neach frame: BeginFrame → input → enabled Engine systems → Update → world changes apply → clear → Render\nStop → Engine cleanup</code></pre><p>Configure runs before the window exists. The Engine reads <code>config.systems</code>, creates the selected built-in systems, and then calls Start. BeginFrame runs before incoming input. Engine processes input and runs enabled movement, animation, collision, keyboard, camera, and projectile-lifetime systems before your Update function.</p><p>Use Start for assets, world size, and objects. Use Update for rules not already covered by Engine, including custom systems. Render is for game-specific drawing; Engine clears and presents the screen. Stop is for game cleanup. Created and destroyed entities become fully applied after Update.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'config'), {content:`<pre><code>GameConfig Game::Configure() const {\n  GameConfig config;\n  config.title = "My Gobo Game";\n  config.window.windowWidth = 1280;\n  config.window.windowHeight = 720;\n  config.render.renderWidth = 1280;\n  config.render.renderHeight = 720;\n  config.systems.useRenderCollisionSystem = false;\n  config.clearColor = {30, 45, 70, 255};\n  return config;\n}</code></pre><h2>Choose Engine systems here</h2><p><code>config.systems</code> controls whether Engine creates and updates rendering, movement, animation, collision, keyboard control, camera movement, projectile lifetime, and collision-debug rendering. They are all enabled by default. Set a flag to false for a system your game does not use; you no longer need to add or update those standard systems yourself.</p><p>Defaults: title Untitled Gobo Game; 1920×1080 window; 1280×720 render view; camera at (0,0); VSync on; 60 FPS cap; clear color (21,21,21,255). <code>targetFPS</code> must be greater than zero.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'context'), {content:`<p>GameContext provides <code>GetWorld()</code>, <code>GetAssets()</code>, <code>GetEvents()</code>, <code>GetCamera()</code>, <code>GetRenderer()</code>, <code>GetSDLRenderer()</code>, <code>FindObject(name)</code>, and <code>RequestQuit()</code>.</p><p>Use World to create and find named objects; Assets to load textures; Camera to change the view; FindObject to retrieve a named Actor/Character as an EntityObject pointer; and RequestQuit to end cleanly. The context is supplied only while the game is running—do not save it after Stop.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'entities'), {content:`<p>Use a plain Entity for simple data-only objects such as map tiles. Give it a name if you need to find it later:</p><pre><code>Entity tile = world.CreateAutoNamedEntity("tile");\ntile.Group("tiles");\ntile.AddComponent&lt;TransformComponent&gt;(Vector2(0, 0));\ntile.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;("terrain", 32, 32);</code></pre><p>Use <code>CreateActor</code> or <code>CreateCharacter</code> for named game objects with callbacks and helpful accessors. Tags are unique labels; groups can contain multiple entities. An entity currently has one tag and one group, so set them deliberately during creation.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'actors'), {content:`<p>An Actor is a named game object with Transform and RigidBody already attached. Create either the basic Actor or your own class derived from it:</p><pre><code>Actor::Config radarConfig;\nradarConfig.position = Vector2(1100, 10);\nradarConfig.scale = Vector2(2, 2);\nActor&amp; radar = world.CreateActor&lt;Radar&gt;("radar", radarConfig);</code></pre><p>Actor provides <code>GetLocation</code>/<code>SetLocation</code>, <code>GetScale</code>/<code>SetScale</code>, <code>GetRotation</code>/<code>SetRotation</code>, and <code>GetVelocity</code>/<code>SetVelocity</code>. Use a derived Actor’s <code>OnCreated()</code> to add its own sprite or other components, as ExampleGame does for Radar.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'characters'), {content:`<p>Character extends Actor with Sprite, BoxCollision, Animation, and Health. For a game-specific player or enemy, derive a class, inherit its constructors, keep a Character::Config, and apply it in OnCreated:</p><pre><code>class Player : public Character {\npublic:\n  using Character::Character;\n  Character::Config config{\n    .actor = {.position = Vector2(100, 100)},\n    .collision = {.width = 32, .height = 32},\n    .sprite = {.assetID = "player", .width = 32, .height = 32}\n  };\n  void OnCreated() override {\n    ApplyCharacterConfig(config);\n    Tag("player");\n  }\n};\nPlayer&amp; player = world.CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player");</code></pre><p>Use <code>GetSprite()</code>, <code>GetAnimation()</code>, <code>GetBoxCollision()</code>, and <code>GetHealth()</code> when a subclass needs its supplied data. Character does not impose controls, attacks, AI, or damage rules; add those in callbacks or game-owned systems.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'systems'), {content:`<p>Choose standard systems in <code>GameConfig.systems</code>. Engine creates them before Start and runs them every frame before your Game::Update. All eight are on by default: rendering, movement, animation, collision, keyboard control, camera movement, projectile lifetime, and collision-debug rendering.</p><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>What it does</th><th>Needed components</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Render</td><td>Draws visible sprites</td><td>Transform + Sprite</td></tr><tr><td>Movement</td><td>Moves by velocity</td><td>Transform + RigidBody</td></tr><tr><td>Animation</td><td>Advances spritesheets</td><td>Sprite + Animation</td></tr><tr><td>Collision</td><td>Detects box overlaps and calls OnCollision</td><td>Transform + BoxCollision</td></tr><tr><td>Keyboard control</td><td>Sends press/release callbacks to controlled objects</td><td>KeyboardControlled + Sprite + RigidBody</td></tr><tr><td>Camera movement</td><td>Follows configured objects within world bounds</td><td>Transform + CameraFollow</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Add only custom systems in Start, then call their Update from Game::Update. ExampleGame’s ProjectileEmitSystem is one such game-owned system.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'input'), {content:`<p>Enable <code>useKeyboardControlSystem</code> (the default) and give an Actor or Character KeyboardControlledComponent, SpriteComponent, and RigidBodyComponent. Engine then sends key-down and key-up events to that object’s callbacks.</p><pre><code>void OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp; event) override {\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_ESCAPE)\n    GetEntity().GetWorld()-&gt;RequestQuit();\n}\nvoid OnKeyReleased(KeyReleasedEvent&amp; event) override {\n  GetRigidBody()-&gt;velocity = Vector2(0, 0);\n}</code></pre><p>This is the preferred object-side workflow for controls. KeyPressedEvent and KeyReleasedEvent each provide the SDL key symbol. Use a custom system or Game callback for input that is not specific to one object. Direct event subscriptions are still available through GameContext for custom behavior, but standard keyboard-controlled objects do not need to subscribe each frame.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'collision'), {content:`<p>Enable collision (the default), then give an object Transform and BoxCollision. Engine checks AABB rectangle overlaps before Game::Update and calls <code>OnCollision(Entity&amp; other)</code> on the object that receives the contact.</p><pre><code>void OnCollision(Entity&amp; other) override {\n  if (other.BelongsToGroup("projectiles")) {\n    GetHealth()-&gt;healthPercentage -= 10;\n    other.Destroy();\n  }\n}</code></pre><p>Contacts repeat while boxes overlap. Width and height use Transform scale; offsets move the box relative to the Transform position. The collision event is also emitted for custom listeners. There are no circle colliders, collision layers/masks, physical pushback, or built-in damage rules.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'first-playable'), {summary:'Create a named Player Character and let configured Engine systems run it.',content:`<div class="notice">The current workflow is object-first: create a named Character subclass, place its setup in OnCreated, and let GameConfig enable the standard systems.</div><h2>1. Make a Player class</h2><p>Create <code>src/Entities/Player.h</code>. Inherit Character and inherit its constructors so World can build it. OnCreated runs automatically after it has been added to the World.</p><pre><code>#pragma once\n#include &lt;GoboEngine.h&gt;\nclass Player : public Character {\npublic:\n  using Character::Character;\n  Character::Config config{\n    .actor = {.position = Vector2(100, 100)},\n    .collision = {.width = 28, .height = 28},\n    .sprite = {.assetID = "player", .width = 32, .height = 32}\n  };\n  void OnCreated() override {\n    ApplyCharacterConfig(config);\n    Tag("player");\n  }\n};</code></pre><h2>2. Load the image and create the named player</h2><p>Include Player.h in Game.cpp. Rendering and movement are enabled by default, so no manual system setup is needed.</p><pre><code>void Game::Start(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  auto&amp; assets = context.GetAssets();\n  if (!assets.AddTexture(context.GetSDLRenderer(),\n        "player", "assets/images/player.png")) {\n    context.RequestQuit(); return;\n  }\n  Player&amp; player = context.GetWorld()\n    .CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player");\n}</code></pre><h2>3. What Engine does each frame</h2><p>Before your Game::Update, Engine moves this Character from its velocity, advances its animation, checks its box collision, processes controls if added, and updates the camera when relevant. RenderSystem draws its Sprite during the render phase. Your Game::Update is for extra rules such as a custom spawner or score system.</p><h2>4. Add behavior to the Player</h2><p>Override <code>OnKeyPressed</code>, <code>OnKeyReleased</code>, or <code>OnCollision</code> inside Player when behavior belongs to the player itself. Add the needed component in OnCreated—for example KeyboardControlledComponent for Engine’s default keyboard dispatch. See Input and Collision for focused examples.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'camera'), {content:`<p>Set the size of your playable level in Start. The enabled CameraMovementSystem uses this boundary to keep the camera in the world.</p><pre><code>context.GetWorld().SetWorldSize(Vector2(1600, 1200));</code></pre><p>Give the player CameraFollowComponent in its OnCreated callback. Engine then follows it automatically before Game::Update:</p><pre><code>AddComponent&lt;CameraFollowComponent&gt;(32, 32);</code></pre><p>RenderSystem subtracts camera position from ordinary sprites, so the world scrolls as the camera moves. A SpriteComponent marked fixed is drawn in screen space instead and is useful for image-based overlays. If camera movement is not useful for your game, disable <code>config.systems.useCameraMovementSystem</code>.</p>`});
designerWiki.pages.push({id:'objects',section:'Gameplay tools',title:'Named game objects and callbacks',summary:'Use named Actor and Character subclasses to give a game object its own behavior.',content:`<p>Actor and Character are EntityObject types: entities with a stable name, convenient component access, and lifecycle callbacks. Create them through World and keep the returned reference only while the object remains alive.</p><pre><code>Player&amp; player = world.CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player");\nEntityObject* found = context.FindObject("player");</code></pre><h2>Callbacks you can override</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Callback</th><th>When it runs</th><th>Good use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OnCreated()</td><td>Once, after creation</td><td>Apply config, add components, tag/group the object</td></tr><tr><td>OnKeyPressed(event)</td><td>On key down for a keyboard-controlled object</td><td>Start movement or perform an action</td></tr><tr><td>OnKeyReleased(event)</td><td>On key up for a keyboard-controlled object</td><td>Stop movement</td></tr><tr><td>OnCollision(other)</td><td>When CollisionSystem finds an overlap</td><td>Damage, pickup, or destroy behavior</td></tr><tr><td>OnDestroyed()</td><td>Provided callback for object cleanup</td><td>Keep cleanup local to the object</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Names must be non-empty and unique. Use <code>CreateAutoNamedObject</code> or <code>CreateAutoNamedEntity</code> when a generated name is better, such as for repeated bullets or tiles.</p>`});
designerWiki.groups[3] = {title:'Presentation',pages:['text','audio']};
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'input'), {content:`<p>Enable <code>useKeyboardControlSystem</code> (the default) and give an Actor or Character KeyboardControlledComponent, SpriteComponent, and RigidBodyComponent. Engine forwards input to that object’s callbacks.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Callback</th><th>When it runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OnKeyPressed(KeyPressedEvent&amp;)</td><td>Once when a key first goes down.</td></tr><tr><td>OnKeyHeld(KeyHeldEvent&amp;)</td><td>Once per held key, every frame.</td></tr><tr><td>OnKeyReleased(KeyReleasedEvent&amp;)</td><td>Once when Engine observes that tracked key go up; all held keys release if the window loses focus.</td></tr><tr><td>OnAllKeys(KeyAllEvent&amp;)</td><td>Once per frame with the set of currently held key symbols.</td></tr></tbody></table><pre><code>void OnKeyHeld(KeyHeldEvent&amp; event) override {\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_RIGHT)\n    GetRigidBody()-&gt;velocity.x = 200;\n}\nvoid OnKeyReleased(KeyReleasedEvent&amp; event) override {\n  if (event.symbol == SDLK_RIGHT)\n    GetRigidBody()-&gt;velocity.x = 0;\n}</code></pre><p>Use object callbacks for object-specific controls. GameContext event subscriptions remain available for game-wide custom behavior, but the built-in keyboard-control workflow subscribes itself and does not need per-frame registration.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'objects'), {content:`<p>Actor and Character are EntityObject types: entities with a stable name, convenient component access, and lifecycle callbacks. Create them through World and keep the returned reference only while the object remains alive.</p><pre><code>Player&amp; player = world.CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player");\nEntityObject* found = context.FindObject("player");</code></pre><h2>Callbacks you can override</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Callback</th><th>When it runs</th><th>Good use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OnCreated()</td><td>Once, after creation</td><td>Apply config, add components, tag/group the object</td></tr><tr><td>OnKeyPressed / OnKeyHeld / OnKeyReleased</td><td>First press, every held-key frame, and release</td><td>Object-specific controls</td></tr><tr><td>OnAllKeys(event)</td><td>Once each frame with the held-key set</td><td>Combined or diagonal input</td></tr><tr><td>OnCollision(other)</td><td>When CollisionSystem finds an overlap</td><td>Damage, pickup, or destroy behavior</td></tr><tr><td>OnDestroyed()</td><td>Provided callback for object cleanup</td><td>Keep cleanup local to the object</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Names must be non-empty and unique. Use <code>CreateAutoNamedObject</code> or <code>CreateAutoNamedEntity</code> when a generated name is better, such as for repeated bullets or tiles.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'limits'), {content:`<h2>What is ready</h2><ul><li>Named Actor and Character objects, object callbacks, texture-backed sprites, 2D movement, camera movement, animation, box collision, keyboard input, basic text labels, and managed audio are available.</li><li>Standard systems are selected in GameConfig and run automatically.</li><li>Vector2 is the normal 2D value type. Division by zero is guarded, but out-of-range vector indexing remains unsafe in release builds.</li></ul><h2>Plan around these boundaries</h2><ul><li>TextLabel is a low-level label primitive; there is no full UI layout, menu, or button system.</li><li>Collision is rectangle-only. There are no circles, radius queries, collision layers/masks, physical pushback, or built-in damage rules.</li><li>Tags are unique and groups can contain many objects, but each object currently has one of each.</li><li>Projectile spawning and combat rules remain game-owned. ExampleGame is a reference, not a drop-in gameplay package.</li></ul>`});
designerWiki.pages.push(
{id:'text',section:'Presentation',title:'Text labels and fonts',summary:'Load a font and render a simple screen-space text label.',content:`<p>AssetManager loads and owns fonts. TextLabel turns a string into a texture and renders it through the current SDL renderer. It is useful for simple HUD readouts such as health or score.</p><pre><code>// In Start\nauto&amp; assets = context.GetAssets();\nassets.AddFont("hud", "assets/fonts/MyFont.ttf", 24.0f);\nlabel = std::make_unique&lt;TextLabel&gt;();\nif (label-&gt;Initialize(context.GetSDLRenderer(),\n    assets.GetFont("hud"), "Score: 0")) {\n  label-&gt;SetPosition(20, 20);\n}\n\n// In Render\nif (label) label-&gt;Render();</code></pre><p>Use SetText, SetColor, SetPosition, and SetScale to update a label. GetWidth and GetHeight include its scale. An empty text string clears its render texture and renders successfully as nothing.</p><div class="notice warning">TextLabel owns an SDL texture but borrows the renderer and font. Destroy or call Shutdown on every label in Game::Stop, while the Engine renderer is still alive. ExampleGame resets its HUD label in Stop.</div>`},
{id:'audio',section:'Presentation',title:'Sound and music',summary:'Load game audio and play it through Engine-managed audio buses.',content:`<p>Application creates, updates, and shuts down AudioManager for each game run. Access it from GameContext; do not create or shut down another AudioManager yourself.</p><pre><code>void Game::Start(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  auto&amp; audio = context.GetAudio();\n  audio.LoadAudio("theme", "assets/sounds/theme.wav");\n  audio.SetBusVolume("Music", 0.5f);\n  audio.PlayMusic("theme", true);\n}</code></pre><p>LoadAudio associates an ID with a file. Play returns an AudioPlaybackID that can be passed to Stop, Pause, or Resume. For common routes, use PlaySFX, PlayMusic, PlayDialogue, or PlayCustom1 through PlayCustom3. The general Play call accepts AudioPlayConfig with a bus, volume, and loop count; zero plays once and minus one loops forever.</p><p>CreateBus configures a named bus with volume, a voice limit, and an overflow policy. SetMasterVolume, SetBusVolume, PauseBus, ResumeBus, and StopBus manage buses after creation.</p>`}
);
// Retain the source-derived article added above instead of the older duplicate.
designerWiki.pages = designerWiki.pages.filter((page, index, pages) =>
  !['tutorial-one-shot-audio', 'audio', 'objects'].includes(page.id) ||
  index === pages.findIndex(candidate => candidate.id === page.id)
);
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'limits'), {content:`<h2>What is ready</h2><ul><li>Named Actor and Character objects, object callbacks, texture-backed sprites, 2D movement, camera movement, animation, box collision, keyboard press/release dispatch, and entity/component systems are available.</li><li>Standard systems are selected in GameConfig and run automatically.</li><li>Vector2 is the normal 2D value type. Avoid unchecked indexes and division by zero.</li></ul><h2>Plan around these boundaries</h2><ul><li>There is no Engine-provided text, font, HUD layout, menu, button, or audio API yet.</li><li>Collision is rectangle-only. There are no circles, radius queries, collision layers/masks, physical pushback, or built-in damage rules.</li><li>Tags are unique and groups can contain many objects, but each object currently has one of each.</li><li>Projectile spawning and combat rules remain game-owned. ExampleGame is a reference, not a drop-in gameplay package.</li></ul>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'lifecycle'), {content:`<pre><code>Configure → Engine prepares selected systems → Start\neach frame: BeginFrame → input → enabled Engine updates → Update → world changes apply → clear → Engine render → Render → present\nStop → Engine cleanup</code></pre><p>Configure runs before the window exists. Engine creates systems selected in GameConfig before Start. Each frame, BeginFrame runs before input; enabled movement, animation, collision, keyboard, camera, and projectile-lifetime systems run before your Update; and World applies deferred changes after Update.</p><p>After clearing, Engine renders sprites automatically, optionally renders collision outlines when debug mode is enabled, and then calls your Render for game-specific drawing. Stop is for game cleanup.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'systems'), {content:`<p>Choose standard systems in <code>GameConfig.systems</code>. Engine creates them before Start and updates them every frame before Game::Update. Rendering is also automatic: Engine draws sprites before your Game::Render callback. The eight flags default to true.</p><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>What it does</th><th>Needed components</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Render</td><td>Draws visible sprites before Game::Render</td><td>Transform + Sprite</td></tr><tr><td>Movement</td><td>Moves by velocity</td><td>Transform + RigidBody</td></tr><tr><td>Animation</td><td>Advances spritesheets</td><td>Sprite + Animation</td></tr><tr><td>Collision</td><td>Detects box overlaps and calls OnCollision</td><td>Transform + BoxCollision</td></tr><tr><td>Keyboard control</td><td>Sends press/held/release callbacks</td><td>KeyboardControlled + Sprite + RigidBody</td></tr><tr><td>Camera movement</td><td>Follows configured objects within world bounds</td><td>Transform + CameraFollow</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Use <code>SetDebugMode(true)</code> from your Game class to draw collision outlines when RenderCollisionSystem is enabled. Add only custom systems in Start, then call their Update from Game::Update.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'context'), {content:`<p>GameContext provides <code>GetWorld()</code>, <code>GetAssets()</code>, <code>GetEvents()</code>, <code>GetAudio()</code>, <code>GetCamera()</code>, <code>GetRenderer()</code>, <code>GetSDLRenderer()</code>, <code>FindObject(name)</code>, and <code>RequestQuit()</code>.</p><p>Use World to create and find named objects; Assets to load textures and fonts; Audio to load and play game sound; Camera to change the view; and RequestQuit to end cleanly. These references belong to Engine and are valid only while the game is running.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'limits'), {content:`<h2>What is ready</h2><ul><li>Named Actor and Character objects, object callbacks, texture-backed sprites, 2D movement, camera movement, animation, box collision, keyboard input, basic text labels, and managed audio are available.</li><li>Standard systems are selected in GameConfig and run automatically.</li><li>Vector2 is the normal 2D value type. Division by zero is guarded, but out-of-range vector indexing remains unsafe in release builds.</li></ul><h2>Plan around these boundaries</h2><ul><li>TextLabel is a low-level label primitive; there is no full UI layout, menu, or button system.</li><li>Collision is rectangle-only. There are no circles, radius queries, collision layers/masks, physical pushback, or built-in damage rules.</li><li>Tags are unique and groups can contain many objects, but each object currently has one of each.</li><li>Projectile spawning and combat rules remain game-owned. ExampleGame is a reference, not a drop-in gameplay package.</li></ul>`});
// 2026-09-23: Engine-managed text replaces directly constructed TextLabel instances.
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'context'), {content:`<p>GameContext provides <code>GetWorld()</code>, <code>GetAssets()</code>, <code>GetEvents()</code>, <code>GetAudio()</code>, <code>GetText()</code>, <code>GetCamera()</code>, <code>GetViewSize()</code>, <code>FindObject(name)</code>, and <code>RequestQuit()</code>.</p><p>Use World to create named objects; Assets to load textures and optional fonts; Audio to play game sound; Text to create and update labels; and GetViewSize when placing screen-space UI. These Engine-owned services are valid only while the game is running.</p>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'text'), {title:'Text labels and fonts',summary:'Create and update Engine-managed screen-space labels by ID.',content:`<p>Use <code>context.GetText()</code> to manage labels. TextManager owns every TextLabel and Engine renders them after your Game::Render callback. You do not construct, render, or shut down TextLabel yourself.</p><pre><code>// In Start\nTextLabelConfig health{\n  .text = "Health: 100",\n  .position = Vector2(20, 20),\n  .color = Color{255, 255, 255, 255},\n  .layer = 100\n};\ncontext.GetText().Create("hud.health", health);\n\n// In Update\ncontext.GetText().SetText("hud.health", "Health: 75");</code></pre><p>Each label has a unique ID. Create returns false for an empty or duplicate ID, an unavailable font, or a rendering failure. Use SetText, SetFont, SetColor, SetPosition, SetScale, SetLayer, SetVisible, Remove, Clear, Has, and Count to manage labels. Labels render from lower layer to higher layer; labels with the same layer preserve creation order.</p><h2>Fonts</h2><p>TextLabelConfig defaults to Engine’s embedded <code>gobo.font.default</code> font. To use another font, add it through AssetManager and set <code>fontID</code> in the config. IDs beginning with <code>gobo.</code> are reserved, and the embedded default font cannot be removed.</p><div class="notice">Do not retain a TextLabel or call a manual Shutdown method—the public API is TextManager only. Engine clears labels before it releases fonts and the renderer.</div>`});
Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === 'assets'), {content:`<h2>Textures</h2><pre><code>void Game::Start(GameContext&amp; context) {\n  context.GetAssets().AddTexture("hero", "assets/images/hero.png");\n}</code></pre><p>AddTexture returns false if loading fails or an ID is already used. The renderer is now owned by AssetManager’s Engine setup, so game code supplies only the asset ID and file path. Use the ID on SpriteComponent. Missing texture IDs are skipped and logged.</p><h2>Fonts</h2><p>Use AddFont(id, filepath, pointSize) for a game font, then reference its ID in TextLabelConfig. The embedded <code>gobo.font.default</code> font is already available for ordinary labels; its reserved ID cannot be replaced or removed.</p>`});

// ExampleGame-based tutorials for designers who want complete, composable patterns.
designerWiki.groups.splice(1, 0, {title:'ExampleGame tutorials',pages:['examplegame-tour','tutorial-player','tutorial-world','tutorial-hud-audio','tutorial-custom-system','tutorial-one-shot-audio']});
const pushDesignerPage = designerWiki.pages.push.bind(designerWiki.pages);
designerWiki.pages.push = (...pages) => {
  const result = pushDesignerPage(...pages.filter(page => !designerWiki.pages.some(existing => existing.id === page.id)));
  for (const [id, override] of Object.entries(sceneDocumentationOverrides))
    Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === id), override);
  for (const [id, override] of Object.entries(spriteTutorialOverrides))
    Object.assign(designerWiki.pages.find(page => page.id === id), override);
  if (!designerWiki.groups.some(group => group.title === 'Input & interaction'))
    designerWiki.groups.splice(3, 0, {title:'Input & interaction',pages:['input','mouse-input','events-guide']});
  if (!designerWiki.groups.some(group => group.title === 'Artwork & animation'))
    designerWiki.groups.splice(5, 0, {title:'Artwork & animation',pages:['assets','sprites-animation','camera']});
  return result;
};
designerWiki.pages.push(
{id:'examplegame-tour',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Read ExampleGame as a blueprint',summary:'Map the supplied example to the parts of your own game project.',content:`<p><code>Projects/ExampleGame</code> is a reference game, not a package to edit or depend on. Its useful patterns are separated into small files that you can reproduce in your own project.</p><table><thead><tr><th>ExampleGame part</th><th>Pattern to reuse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ExampleGame.cpp::Configure</td><td>Window, render view, frame, and clear-color choices.</td></tr><tr><td>ExampleGame.cpp::Start</td><td>Load assets, create HUD text, set world size, and create named objects.</td></tr><tr><td>Entities/player.h</td><td>A Character subclass that configures itself and owns its controls/collision response.</td></tr><tr><td>Entities/tank.h and truck.h</td><td>Small enemy subclasses with different config and grouped behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>Entities/radar.h</td><td>A fixed-screen Actor used as a simple HUD image.</td></tr><tr><td>ExampleGame.cpp::Update</td><td>Run a game-owned system and refresh a text label from a named player.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Start by copying the structure—not its art paths, IDs, projectile rules, or map-file parser. Those are specific to the example.</p>`},
{id:'tutorial-player',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Tutorial: a controllable Character',summary:'Build a Player class using Character, OnCreated, and combined held-key input.',content:`<h2>1. Define the object</h2><p>Create <code>src/Entities/Player.h</code>. Like ExampleGame, inherit Character and inherit its constructors so World can create it. Put setup in OnCreated instead of Game::Start.</p><pre><code>class Player : public Character {\npublic:\n  using Character::Character;\n  Character::Config config{\n    .actor = {.position = Vector2(100, 100)},\n    .collision = {.width = 32, .height = 32},\n    .sprite = {.assetID = "player", .width = 32, .height = 32}\n  };\n  void OnCreated() override {\n    ApplyCharacterConfig(config);\n    Tag("player");\n    AddComponent&lt;KeyboardControlledComponent&gt;(\n      Vector2(0, -200), Vector2(200, 0),\n      Vector2(0, 200), Vector2(-200, 0));\n    AddComponent&lt;CameraFollowComponent&gt;(16, 16);\n  }\n};</code></pre><h2>2. Support diagonal movement</h2><p>ExampleGame uses OnAllKeys so every frame starts from zero velocity and combines held directions. This avoids a released key stopping a different still-held direction.</p><pre><code>void OnAllKeys(KeyAllEvent&amp; event) override {\n  auto* control = GetComponent&lt;KeyboardControlledComponent&gt;();\n  auto* body = GetRigidBody();\n  if (!control || !body) return;\n  Vector2 velocity(0);\n  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_UP)) velocity += control-&gt;upVelocity;\n  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_RIGHT)) velocity += control-&gt;rightVelocity;\n  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_DOWN)) velocity += control-&gt;downVelocity;\n  if (event.heldKeys.contains(SDLK_LEFT)) velocity += control-&gt;leftVelocity;\n  body-&gt;velocity = velocity;\n}</code></pre><h2>3. Create it</h2><pre><code>context.GetAssets().AddTexture("player", "assets/images/player.png");\nPlayer&amp; player = context.GetWorld().CreateCharacter&lt;Player&gt;("player");</code></pre><p>KeyboardControlSystem is enabled by default. It forwards KeyAllEvent only to objects with KeyboardControlledComponent, SpriteComponent, and RigidBodyComponent—Character supplies the last two.</p>`},
{id:'tutorial-world',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Tutorial: build a small scrolling world',summary:'Create tiles, set world bounds, follow the player, and add fixed-screen art.',content:`<h2>1. Load a tile image and make tiles</h2><p>ExampleGame reads positions from a map file, then creates one plain Entity per tile. You can begin with a simple loop instead:</p><pre><code>auto&amp; world = context.GetWorld();\ncontext.GetAssets().AddTexture("ground", "assets/images/ground.png");\nfor (int y = 0; y &lt; 10; ++y) {\n  for (int x = 0; x &lt; 15; ++x) {\n    Entity tile = world.CreateAutoNamedEntity("tile");\n    tile.Group("tiles");\n    tile.AddComponent&lt;TransformComponent&gt;(Vector2(x * 64, y * 64));\n    tile.AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;("ground", 32, 32, -1);\n  }\n}\nworld.SetWorldSize(Vector2(960, 640));</code></pre><p>Use lower sprite layers for terrain so characters draw over it. Set the world size to the actual playable boundary; CameraMovementSystem uses it to clamp the view.</p><h2>2. Add a fixed UI image</h2><p>Radar in ExampleGame is an Actor that adds a fixed Sprite in OnCreated. Fixed sprites ignore camera movement:</p><pre><code>class Radar : public Actor {\npublic:\n  using Actor::Actor;\n  void OnCreated() override {\n    AddComponent&lt;SpriteComponent&gt;(\n      "radar", 64, 64, 2, true);\n  }\n};\nActor::Config ui{.position = Vector2(20, 20)};\nworld.CreateActor&lt;Radar&gt;("radar", ui);</code></pre><p>Use <code>context.GetViewSize()</code> when positioning a fixed item against the right or bottom edge.</p>`},
{id:'tutorial-hud-audio',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Tutorial: a health HUD with sound',summary:'Create managed text, update it from Character health, and start a looped sound.',content:`<h2>1. Create the label in Start</h2><pre><code>TextLabelConfig health{\n  .text = "Health: 100",\n  .position = Vector2(20, 20),\n  .color = Color{255, 255, 255, 255},\n  .layer = 100\n};\ncontext.GetText().Create("hud.health", health);</code></pre><p>TextManager owns this label and renders it automatically. The default font is used when fontID is omitted.</p><h2>2. Refresh the label in Update</h2><pre><code>Character* player = context.GetWorld().FindCharacter("player");\nif (player && player-&gt;GetHealth()) {\n  context.GetText().SetText("hud.health",\n    "Health: " + std::to_string(player-&gt;GetHealth()-&gt;healthPercentage));\n}</code></pre><p>The name must match the name used when you created the Player. Checking for null keeps the UI safe if the player was removed.</p><h2>3. Load and play audio</h2><pre><code>auto&amp; audio = context.GetAudio();\naudio.LoadAudio("ambient", "assets/sounds/ambient.wav", true);\naudio.SetBusVolume("SFX", 0.30f);\naudio.Play("ambient", {.bus = "SFX", .volume = 1.0f, .loops = -1});</code></pre><p>As in ExampleGame, load and start persistent audio in Start. Engine updates the audio service every frame and shuts it down with the game.</p>`},
{id:'tutorial-custom-system',section:'ExampleGame tutorials',title:'Tutorial: add a game-owned system',summary:'Use a custom system for rules that are specific to your game.',content:`<p>ProjectileEmitSystem in ExampleGame is deliberately game-specific. This is the right pattern for mechanics that do not belong in the reusable Engine.</p><h2>1. Add and subscribe in Start</h2><pre><code>auto&amp; world = context.GetWorld();\nworld.AddSystem&lt;MySpawnSystem&gt;();\nif (auto* system = world.GetSystem&lt;MySpawnSystem&gt;())\n  system-&gt;SubscribeToEvents(context.GetEvents());</code></pre><h2>2. Update it from Game::Update</h2><pre><code>void Game::Update(GameContext&amp; context, float deltaSeconds) {\n  if (auto* system = context.GetWorld().GetSystem&lt;MySpawnSystem&gt;())\n    system-&gt;Update(context.GetWorld());\n}</code></pre><p>Keep your custom system in your project’s <code>src/Systems</code> folder and add it to your project CMake source list. Give it only the components and event subscriptions it truly needs. Do not modify Engine’s built-in systems to add one game’s shooting, scoring, or enemy rules.</p>`}
);
